export const debate = {"id":"vela-dillahunty-transcendental-evidence-god-2020","number":"143","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Tyler Vela vs Matt Dillahunty: Is There Good Evidence for God?","label":"Transcendental logic and divine evidence","date":"2026-08-23","duration":"2 hr 17 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kf0Us__r9SQ","motion":"Do laws of logic and other transcendental facts provide good evidence for a necessary personal God?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Vela grounds transcendental facts in a necessary personal mind; Dillahunty argues that the inference remains underspecified, comparatively unsupported, and evidentially nondiscriminating.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"I'm asking the the meta question","context":"Vela distinguishes agreement about the reliable operation of transcendental facts from his further metaphysical question about what grounds them."},"con":{"text":"does this reliably point to the proposition that a god is the explanation","context":"Dillahunty frames good evidence as support that discriminates reliably in favor of a sufficiently specific divine explanation."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Transcendental theism","speaker":"Tyler Vela","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Evidential skepticism","speaker":"Matt Dillahunty","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":76,"con":84},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-grounding-status","title":"Whether Transcendental Facts Require a Ground","timebox":"11:48–118:14","score":{"pro":80,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-necessary-ground-not-brute-fact","time":"11:48","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Ignorance or brute-fact appeals do not remove the explanatory demand, whereas God is proposed as a self-existing necessary reality rather than an unexplained contingent posit.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move puts the grounding demand at the debate’s center and distinguishes the proposed divine ground from a contingent posit, establishing a relevant constructive route. Principal limitation: Describing God as self-existing and necessary does not establish why that description answers an explanatory request unavailable to logical facts themselves, while brute fact, necessity, and self-existence remain insufficiently differentiated. Live burden: The argument must provide a reason necessary logical relations cannot be fundamental, explain what work divine necessity performs, and avoid granting God an exemption through redescriptive terminology. Locked score: The mixed band records relevance and a thesis alongside materially overstated necessity, limited evidential warrant, incomplete engagement with necessary-brute alternatives, and an explanatory asymmetry that remains unsupported.","tags":[{"label":"Special pleading","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/","context":"The divine posit receives explanatory termination through necessity without a developed reason that equally necessary logical facts cannot terminate explanation."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-logic-independent-of-god","time":"32:56","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Basic logical laws appear necessarily true whether or not God exists, and no personal or transcendent foundation has been demonstrated as necessary for their truth.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly contests personal dependence by treating identity and noncontradiction as necessary across divine and nondivine possibilities, thereby engaging the grounding contention without disputing logic’s reality. Principal limitation: Imaginability remains an unreliable guide to metaphysical possibility, and asserting that logical truths persist without God neither supplies a truthmaker account nor establishes that divine nature cannot explain their necessity. Live burden: The position must clarify whether logic is fundamental, structural, conceptual, or descriptive, then demonstrate why positing a personal ground provides no explanatory improvement instead of merely restating independence. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct responsiveness, modal qualification, coherence, and pressure on divine dependence, while withholding credit pending a rigorous account of necessity and explanation.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-function-does-not-answer-grounding","time":"117:19","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Agreement that transcendental facts operate reliably does not answer the distinct meta-level question of what explains or grounds the facts themselves.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply distinguishes agreement about transcendental facts from the meta-level request for their explanation, diagnosing why functional description may leave the grounding question substantively unanswered. Principal limitation: Identifying an unanswered question does not establish that ontological grounding is necessary, much less that the ground is personal, necessary, or divine, so the positive inference remains unestablished. Live burden: The pro side must justify the explanatory demand and demonstrate that descriptive or fundamentalist accounts are incomplete, rather than redescribing the request at another conceptual level. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional clarity, calibration, charitable representation, and contact with the immediate reply, while preserving the qualification that articulating the meta-question cannot itself answer it.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-logic-descriptive-no-further-ground","time":"37:59","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Logical and mathematical laws can describe identity and noncontradiction without requiring an additional entity or foundation to guarantee that their relations hold.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply separates logical and mathematical relations from a guaranteeing entity, offering an economical alternative to the grounding demand through identity and noncontradiction. Principal limitation: The arithmetic illustration shows that grounding is not indispensable in this case, but it cannot establish that transcendental facts are merely descriptive or that truthmaker, applicability, normativity, and modal questions dissolve identically. Live burden: The position must explain why deeper explanation is unwarranted, clarify the ontological status and applicability of logical relations, and demonstrate that introducing a mind postpones rather than improves explanation. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precision, proportionate calibration, responsiveness, and resistance to explanatory addition, while retaining a limited obligation to generalize beyond the illustrative arithmetic case.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-personal-divine-inference","title":"From Transcendental Facts to a Necessary Personal Mind","timebox":"5:16–69:34","score":{"pro":72,"con":89},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-personal-mind-from-logical-attributes","time":"5:16","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Logical laws possess objective, necessary, timeless, immaterial, and transcendent attributes that are better explained by a transcendent personal mind than by naturalism or Platonism.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move supplies the personal-ground bridge by connecting objective, necessary, timeless, immaterial, transcendent logical attributes to a proposed mind, giving the constructive case direction and relevance. Principal limitation: Its eliminative language compresses transitions, treating problems for naturalism and Platonism as leaving agency without exhausting impersonal alternatives or establishing why abstract necessity, timelessness, or causal inertness indicates personality. Live burden: The case must distinguish grounding from explanation, demonstrate why truths reside in a mind, and compare noncausal Platonist or fundamentalist accounts instead of declaring their failure. Locked score: The mixed band reflects excellent central-burden contact but materially overstated force, incomplete comparative warrant, unstable terminology, and an underdeveloped inference from logical attributes to an omnipotent personal ground.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-vague-divine-hypothesis","time":"24:46","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A generalized classical-theist mind or creator is too vague to function as a discrete hypothesis capable of being reliably favored by unexplained logic or order.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies the underdetermination between attaching classical attributes to divinity and specifying a discriminating hypothesis, challenging the inference from logical facts to a personal God. Principal limitation: It does not rebut every attributed property, while classical theism can present necessary, personal, and maximal characteristics whose evidential implications remain contestable. Live burden: The objection must identify the missing operational or comparative content, show how divine and impersonal alternatives accommodate the same evidence, and distinguish permissible metaphysical abstraction from evidentially disabling vagueness. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional terminological precision, proportionate argumentative force, direct engagement with the central burden, and a coherent discrimination challenge, moderated only by the limited property-specific rebuttal.","tags":[{"label":"Ambiguity effect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/ambiguity-effect/","context":"The criticism materially concerns whether an underspecified divine hypothesis can be compared reliably rather than merely attracting confidence through familiar but vague attributes."}]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-abstract-truths-grounded-in-mind","time":"36:06","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Naturalism allegedly cannot ground transcendental facts, while abstract truths are intelligible as mental concepts and therefore fit more coherently within a transcendent mind.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply engages the independence challenge, coupling the inadequacy of naturalistic grounding with a mind proposal and preserving the contested bridge from transcendental truths to personal explanation. Principal limitation: Reiterating naturalism’s epistemological failure establishes neither that abstract truths are mental nor that a transcendent mind follows, while conflating conceptual apprehension, propositions, truthmakers, and ontological foundations. Live burden: The argument must distinguish truths from thoughts concerning truths, explain how divine mentality secures objective necessity without voluntarism, and evaluate the strongest mind-independent realist alternatives. Locked score: The mixed band reflects direct relevance and a recoverable answer, yet the positive inference remains substantially compressed, the eliminative comparison incomplete, and the evidential warrant disproportionate to the claimed explanatory superiority.","tags":[{"label":"Equivocation","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/","context":"The inference risks shifting between truths as abstract realities and concepts as objects of thought when moving from intelligibility to a mental ground."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-anomaly-does-not-identify-yahweh","time":"67:47","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Even an extraordinary celestial message could support generic agency without independently establishing Yahweh, necessity, personality, or another specific deity while rival explanations remain.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply discriminates evidential relevance, confirmation, sufficient conviction, and identification with precision, conceding that an anomaly could support a generic god hypothesis while refusing an inference to Yahweh. Principal limitation: Although it invokes unresolved alternatives, it leaves their plausibility schematic and does not explain how strongly the observation would update toward agency or which discriminating evidence would narrow the candidate field. Live burden: The case must identify bridging premises to infer necessity, personality, and specificity, then show why the transcendental considerations cannot provide those connections. Locked score: The very strong band reflects careful calibration, engagement, qualifications, and evidential concessions, tempered only by the absence of a fuller comparative explanation of remaining hypotheses.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-abduction-alternatives","title":"Abduction, Alternatives, and Comparative Explanation","timebox":"13:56–114:51","score":{"pro":74,"con":81},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-unified-divine-explanation","time":"13:56","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"A single divine ground can unify transcendental and other facts through coherence, parsimony, explanatory power, explanatory scope, and comparatively low ad hocness.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move usefully shifts from bare compatibility to familiar explanatory virtues, presenting the divine hypothesis as a unified account with coherence, scope, power, and comparatively low ad hocness across multiple facts. Principal limitation: It declares God exemplary across this matrix without conducting the necessary side-by-side comparisons, and its expansion from transcendental facts to many features of reality enlarges the claim beyond the supplied support. Live burden: The proposal must measure ontological and explanatory costs, address whether a personal divine mind is itself complex, and compare concrete naturalist, Platonist, and fundamentalist candidates under consistent criteria. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a coherent and highly relevant comparative framework whose conclusion remains materially overstated because the asserted parsimony and superiority are announced rather than demonstrated against the strongest available rivals.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-abduction-incomplete-candidate-set","time":"22:42","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Abduction becomes unreliable when its candidate set is poorly bounded, because compatibility with observations does not establish that an extraordinary hypothesis is the likeliest explanation.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move challenges the theistic abduction and distinguishes reasonable ordinary inference from confidence in extraordinary candidates when the possibility space and alternatives remain understood. Principal limitation: Its claim of unreliability exceeds what the analogy establishes, because abduction can responsibly rank known explanations without an exhaustive candidate list when background frequencies and comparative expectations are independently justified. Live burden: The objection must specify the candidate set’s incompleteness, identify genuinely live omitted rivals, and explain why uncertainty about unknown possibilities materially weakens this divine comparison rather than every abductive inference. Locked score: The strong band reflects clear responsiveness, calibrated acknowledgment of ordinary cases, and substantial underdetermination pressure, moderated by an imprecise completeness threshold and no developed alternative account.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-unspecified-alternative-not-defeater","time":"113:41","role":"Supporting reply","words":"A merely unspecified possible explanation does not defeat an abductive case unless a substantive competing account or a concrete objection to the offered comparison is supplied.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply usefully distinguishes an unsupported possibility from a substantive defeater, preserving abductive inference against paralysis when an interlocutor gestures toward an explanation without developing its relevance. Principal limitation: It treats candidate-set incompleteness lightly, because priors, selection effects, causal baselines, or hypothesis spaces can reduce confidence even before an opponent supplies a replacement, and it therefore misses the comparative-data objection. Live burden: The pro side must establish the inference’s strength, explain when unknown alternatives become negligible, and compare impersonal or naturalistic accounts without transferring the justificatory burden. Locked score: The strong band recognizes support for a subsidiary defeater principle, while preserving deductions for partial responsiveness, slightly overstated force, qualification, and inadequate engagement with underdetermination.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-abduction-comparative-data-gap","time":"26:42","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A supernatural abduction lacks warrant without well-bounded candidates, comparative likelihood information, or confirmed divine causation, and cannot follow merely from missing natural explanations.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply challenges both negative elimination and comparative warrant, explaining that the absence of an accepted natural account cannot independently provide likelihood evidence favoring supernatural causation. Principal limitation: Its near-exhaustive-candidate requirement remains insufficiently delimited, while the suggestion that good supernatural evidence may be unavailable overreaches the narrower, better-supported criticism of this abduction. Live burden: The skeptical case must specify sufficient comparative evidence, separate principled uncertainty from categorical exclusion, and identify credible candidate classes or predictive differences instead of relying upon unavailable data. Locked score: The strong band reflects substantial relevance, persuasive resistance to premature supernatural inference, and valuable attention to causal baselines, tempered by overbroad framing, inadequately justified universal requirements, and no complete positive alternative.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-good-evidence-standard","title":"Standards for Good Evidence and Sound Inference","timebox":"16:42–73:43","score":{"pro":75,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-philosophical-abduction-as-evidence","time":"50:16","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Deductive implication and abductive premise support can constitute philosophical evidence, while restricting evidence to directly repeatable empirical observations would be self-undermining.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply defends implication and abduction as legitimate evidentiary methods, answering the demand for warranted premises and an excessively restrictive interpretation of repeatable evidence. Principal limitation: It treats logical positivism and preference for direct empirical evidence as interchangeable, exceeding what the opposing formulation warrants, while characterizing the argument as sound before establishing its disputed grounding and comparative premises. Live burden: The defense must progress beyond methodological admissibility and demonstrate that this abduction employs specified hypotheses, justified priors, and comparative advantages over serious alternatives. Locked score: The strong/competent band reflects responsive engagement, rejection of crude verificationism, and clarification of evidential categories, tempered by representational overreach and inadequate demonstration that the admitted methodology supports the proposed divine conclusion.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evidence-reliable-discrimination","time":"16:42","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Good evidence should reduce bias and reliably favor a sufficiently specific, testable God hypothesis through repeatability, independent verification, falsifiability, or causal relevance.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move presents a disciplined evidentiary standard emphasizing reliable discrimination, specificity, independent confirmation, and bias reduction, while appropriately treating repeatability and falsifiability as ideals rather than exceptionless requirements. Principal limitation: It does not individually examine each explanatory virtue claimed for the divine account, and metaphysical evidence can remain rationally relevant without experimental repetition, causal isolation, or verification. Live burden: The standard must accommodate philosophical and historical evidence, identify sufficiently specific divine predictions, and demonstrate why the transcendental considerations fail comparatively instead of merely falling short of experimental ideals. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise qualification, burden relevance, coherent hypothesis-sensitive reasoning, and charitable accommodation of nonexperimental evidence, tempered by incomplete application to the constructive case.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-unattainable-skeptical-link-standard","time":"71:10","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Requiring an incontrovertible link to God makes success unattainable if naturalistic, unknown, or brute-fact alternatives are always permitted regardless of the observation.","score":69,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move identifies a legitimate methodological danger: an evidentiary standard becomes unfairly insulated if every possible observation can be dismissed through an unspecified naturalistic, brute-fact, or unknown alternative without comparative assessment. Principal limitation: It shifts between reliable support, confirmation, and an incontrovertible link, overlooking the opponent's explicit concession that evidence may count toward God without uniquely identifying Yahweh or eliminating every residual possibility. Live burden: The reply must address the actual graded-discrimination standard, show where its threshold becomes excessive, and demonstrate that the pro evidence improves the divine hypothesis relative to alternatives rather than demanding their complete exclusion. Locked score: The mixed band reflects relevant pressure on skeptical methodology but only partial responsiveness, unstable evidential terminology, materially overstated impossibility, and insufficient charity toward the stated concession.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-soundness-requires-supported-premises","time":"49:42","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A valid argument is not good evidence for God unless its premises are supported as true, because the disputed conclusion requires soundness rather than formal validity alone.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply distinguishes validity from soundness and explains that correct inference cannot establish the debated conclusion unless its premises receive adequate evidentiary support, reinforcing the central burden. Principal limitation: It neither identifies the particular unsuccessful premise nor evaluates the offered abductive defenses individually, while actual soundness remains distinguishable from the accessible question whether available evidence rationally supports premise truth. Live burden: The skeptical side must separately examine the grounding, personal-mind, and comparative premises, then specify what proportionate evidentiary support would render each reasonable without demanding certainty. Locked score: The very strong band reflects coherent logical discrimination, relevance, matched argumentative force, and a decisive challenge to unsupported premises, moderated only by insufficient premise-specific application.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-revelation-hiddenness","title":"Divine Revelation, Hiddenness, and Evidential Expectations","timebox":"27:59–132:08","score":{"pro":82,"con":76},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-cumulative-public-revelation","time":"128:37","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Creation, morality, transcendental facts, special revelation, Jesus, and the resurrection collectively form publicly available evidence against the claim that God remains hidden.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply contests hiddenness through a cumulative catalogue of alleged disclosures spanning natural, moral, transcendental, historical, and revelatory domains, rather than depending upon private experience. Principal limitation: Enumerating categories establishes neither their reliability, independence, clarity, nor discriminatory power, while claiming adequacy for every reasonable person substantially understates informed disagreement and competing explanations. Live burden: The cumulative case must evaluate interdependence among components, substantiate its historical and theological premises, compare religiously plural interpretations, and explain reasonable nonbelief despite sufficient disclosure. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct responsiveness, explanatory breadth, and a cumulative strategy, tempered by materially overstated force, compressed public-warrant assertions, limited component-level substantiation, and incomplete engagement with the strongest ambiguity objection.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-hiddenness-evidential-expectation","time":"27:59","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Persistent obscurity and disagreement about unfalsifiable God claims conflict with the clarity reasonably expected if a capable classical-theist God wanted its existence widely known.","score":66,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move develops a transcript-grounded evidential expectation from divine capability and prospective self-disclosure, supplying materially relevant counterpressure against an uncomplicated personal-God hypothesis without merely denying transcendental grounding. Principal limitation: It never engages the decisive inference from logical attributes to a personal ground, while its debate-elimination prediction relies on substantially underspecified assumptions concerning divine intentions, noncoercive freedom, epistemic distance, and permissible ambiguity. Live burden: The hiddenness argument must identify the relevant disclosure intention, predict the expected distribution of reasonable nonbelief, and differentiate evidential insufficiency from persistent disagreement compatible with adequate revelation. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes genuine relevance to divine expectations, but also a targeted-grounding nonanswer, incomplete predictive specification, and an overextended inference from disagreement alone.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-revelation-obligation-not-compulsion","time":"131:41","role":"Major direct reply","words":"God may reveal enough to impose an obligation to know without intending to compel every person to acquire settled conviction or actual belief.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply distinguishes universal revelation and epistemic obligation from settled knowledge, conviction, or compelled belief, correcting the attributed divine objective and defeating the opponent’s conditional as presented. Principal limitation: This reframing does not establish that the revelation is clear, accessible, or equitable to create obligation, while reasonable nonbelief can still challenge universal opportunity without implying compulsion. Live burden: The pro side must specify the epistemic threshold for obligation, demonstrate that creation and other disclosures satisfy it across circumstances, and address sincere nonbelief under this narrower formulation. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional precision, proportionate calibration, justified reframing, and decisive responsiveness, while preserving the downstream question whether the available evidence actually grounds the asserted obligation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unfulfilled-divine-disclosure-aim","time":"131:27","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If God desires universal knowledge and possesses an effective means to secure it, persistent nonbelief counts against attributing that unfulfilled intention to God.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply constructs a conditional test connecting universal divine intention, effective disclosure, and nonfulfillment, thereby making its evidential implication transparent, proportionate, and responsive. Principal limitation: It does not independently establish that actual knowledge is the relevant divine objective or that disclosure guarantees uptake, so the inference weakens if revelation instead seeks opportunity, responsibility, or noncompelled relationship. Live burden: The skeptical side must substantiate the attributed intention, distinguish knowledge from compelled conviction, and demonstrate that reasonable nonbelief contradicts the narrower disclosure objective actually defended. Locked score: The very strong band reflects explicit qualification, excellent coherence, decisive burden contact, and charitable inferential confinement, while locating the remaining vulnerability in contestable conditional antecedents rather than internal structure.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":76,"strengths":["Clearly separated the operation of transcendental facts from the further meta-level grounding question.","Presented a unified explanatory framework using coherence, scope, power, parsimony, and low ad hocness.","Correctly defended philosophical and abductive reasoning as potentially legitimate evidence rather than requiring laboratory repetition.","Precisely distinguished revelation sufficient for obligation from an intention to compel universal settled belief."],"blunders":[{"text":"The central move from logical attributes to a personal mind did not exhaust impersonal alternatives or establish that abstract truth must be mental.","links":[]},{"text":"The necessary-ground argument gave God explanatory termination without adequately showing why necessary logical facts could not be similarly fundamental.","links":[{"label":"Special pleading","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"}]},{"text":"The cumulative revelation case listed evidence categories without establishing their independence, reliability, or power to resolve reasonable disagreement.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":84,"strengths":["Required evidence to discriminate reliably in favor of a sufficiently specific hypothesis.","Exposed the incomplete candidate set and missing comparative likelihood information in the divine abduction.","Distinguished evidence toward generic agency from confirmation of Yahweh or a necessary personal deity.","Offered a clear descriptive alternative on which logical relations need no additional guaranteeing entity.","Kept the disclosure objection explicitly conditional on a universal divine aim and effective mechanism."],"blunders":[{"text":"The hiddenness argument did not answer the immediate inference from logical attributes to a personal ground and left divine aims underspecified.","links":[]},{"text":"The candidate-set objection sometimes suggested a near-exhaustive comparison was needed without defining a workable threshold for responsible abduction.","links":[]},{"text":"The broader suggestion that good supernatural evidence might be unavailable exceeded the narrower comparative-data objection supported in the exchange.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest theistic case is a modest abductive claim: a necessary rational ground modestly improves the joint explanation of objective modal structure, rational access, and logical normativity.","premises":["Logical norms are not merely descriptions of regular behavior; they govern correct inference across actual and counterfactual reasoning and remain binding when thinkers make mistakes.","An adequate explanation should jointly address logical necessity, normative authority, and the reliable accessibility of logical relations to finite rational agents, not necessity alone.","A necessary rational ground provides a unified model in which logical truths reflect stable rational structure rather than arbitrary divine choices or contingent psychological events.","Theism gains support only if this unified account has lower total theoretical cost and better comparative reach than impersonal realism, structuralism, or accepting logical facts as fundamental."],"conclusion":"Therefore, transcendental facts may provide limited abductive evidence for a necessary rational ground, provided the theistic model outperforms specified impersonal alternatives without relying on elimination alone."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Normative unity argument","text":"Logical truths do more than describe which inferences people happen to make: they distinguish reasoning that should be accepted from reasoning that should be rejected. A necessary rational ground could explain why modal truth, inferential normativity, and rational intelligibility belong to one structure. This does not follow merely from calling logic abstract, and it does not establish a specific religion. It offers a new comparative question: whether impersonal accounts explain normative authority and rational accessibility together as economically as a necessary rational source does."},{"title":"Prediction through cognitive fit","text":"A personal-ground hypothesis can be made more discriminating by deriving a modest expectation: finite rational creatures in an ordered world should possess broadly reliable access to basic logical relations, while retaining fallibility because their cognition is limited. The relevant datum is not simply that logic exists, but that abstract reasoning successfully coordinates inquiry across mathematics, language, and empirical science. This prediction needs comparison with evolutionary and structuralist accounts, yet it gives the hypothesis more content than a vague appeal to an unspecified creator."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Transcendental facts do not presently constitute good evidence for a necessary personal God because the divine hypothesis lacks sufficiently distinctive predictions and demonstrated comparative advantage over impersonal accounts.","premises":["Evidence favors a hypothesis when the observed facts are more expected under that hypothesis than under relevant competitors, not merely when the hypothesis is compatible with them.","Necessary logical relations are compatible with several families of views, including impersonal realism, structuralism, conceptualism, naturalized accounts, and positions treating such relations as fundamental.","The generic necessary-mind hypothesis does not yet specify what logical structure, cognitive accessibility, religious disclosure, or world history should be expected rather than merely permitted.","Without comparative likelihoods or independent evidence of divine mentality, moving from explanatory dissatisfaction with impersonal views to a personal ground risks substituting a label for an explanation."],"conclusion":"Therefore, withholding assent to the personal-God inference is warranted unless a more specific divine model generates distinctive expectations and demonstrably compares better with serious impersonal alternatives."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Model multiplicity argument","text":"A grounding explanation should reduce uncertainty, but adding a necessary mind may multiply unresolved models: Does logic constitute that mind's nature, arise from its thoughts, or constrain it independently? Each option carries different consequences for necessity, arbitrariness, and explanatory priority. Until the theistic account selects and defends one model, apparent unity may conceal several incompatible explanations. This is not an appeal to an unknown alternative; it is an internal model-selection problem showing that the phrase personal ground does not yet determine how the proposed explanation works."},{"title":"Common-data symmetry","text":"Both theism and impersonal realism begin with the same data: logical necessity, objective validity, and human access to basic inference. If each view can accommodate those facts, the evidence discriminates only when one predicts them with fewer auxiliary assumptions or independently supported mechanisms. Theistic language about rationality may make the fit feel intuitive, but intuition is not yet a likelihood comparison. The skeptical case can therefore grant the full transcendental data while asking for an asymmetry that favors personality rather than merely restating the data in personal terms."}]}},"year":2020};
